body:
I can't seem to find anything that explains how a script element created in a custom element and added to that element's shadow DOM can find its "owning" element, or even just access the shadow document it get added to.

For example:

```js
customElements.define('my-element',
  class extends HTMLElement {
    connectedCallback() {
      window.customElementInstance = this;
      const script = document.createElement(`script`);
      script.id = `testid`;
      script.textContent = `
        (function(scope) {
          console.log(
            scope,
            document,
            document.getElementById(`testid`),
            window.customElementInstance
          );
        })(this)
      `;
      this.attachShadow({mode: 'open'}).append(script);
    }
  }
);
```

This shows that the script's scope is `window`, and it shows a `document` that is the main page's document, not the shadow DOM's document. Of course,  the main document has no record of any element with `id` "testid", since we added it to the shadow DOM, but inspecting the element using dev tools shows there is no `<script>` element in the shadow DOM either, so where does the script element even go? It's not in the main document, it's not in the shadow DOM, but it _does_ exist because it got executed and can access the `window.customElementInstance` to get a reference to its "owner" that way, but that's super hacky.

There does not appear to be any other way for scripts that are added to the shadow DOM to actually work _with_ that shadow DOM... what key information am I missing? =(
